12†Culture Collection, Department of Clinical Bacteriology, University of Göteborg, Sweden】 ^{2} 【13†School of Food Biosciences, University of Reading, Reading, UK】 ^{3} 【14†Infectious Diseases Section, VA Medical Center, West Los Angeles, CA 90073, USA】 ^{4} 【15†Department of Medicine, UCLA School of Medicine, CA 90073, USA】\nL14: \nL15: Correspondence Paul A. Lawson\nL16:  【16†[email protected]】\nL17: \nL18:   * Published: 01 March 2005\nL19:   * 【11†info Info】\nL20:     * 【17†Article information】\nL21:     * 【18†Full-Text】\nL22:     * 【19†Figures and Tables】\nL23:     * 【20†References (27)】\nL24:     * 【21†Cited By (83)】\nL25:     * 【22†Supplementary Material】\nL26:     * 【23†Metrics】\nL27: \nL28:   * 【11†build Tools】\nL29: ### Tools\nL30: \nL31:     * 【11†Add to my favourites】\nL32: \nL33:     * 【11†Create publication alert】\nL34: \nL35: 【11†Create citation alert】\nL36: \nL37: 【11†Create correction alert】\nL38: \nL39:     * 【11†Export citation】\nL40:       * 【24†BibT_{E}X】\nL41:       * 【25†Endnote】\nL42:       * 【24†Zotero】\nL43:       * 【26†Medlars】\nL44:       * 【27†RefWorks】\nL45:       * 【28†Mendeley†www.mendeley.com】\nL46:     * 【11†Recommend to library】\nL47:     *     * [Input]\nL48: ## Abstract\nL49: Phenotypic and phylogenetic studies were performed on two strains of an unidentified Gram-positive, fastidious, non-spore-forming, coccus-shaped bacterium recovered from human blood. The organism was catalase-negative and grew under strictly anaerobic conditions and in the presence of 2 and 6 % O 2.\nL50: Comparative 16S rRNA gene sequencing demonstrated that the unidentified bacterium was, phylogenetically, far removed from peptostreptococci and related Gram-positive coccus-shaped organisms, but exhibited a phylogenetic association with Clostridium rRNA cluster III [as defined by 【29†Collins et al., Int J Syst Bacteriol 44 (1994), 812–826】 ].\nL51: Sequence divergence values of 15 % or more were observed between the unidentified bacterium and all other recognized species within this and related rRNA clostridial clusters. Treeing analysis showed that the unknown bacterium formed a deep line branching at the periphery of rRNA cluster III and represents a hitherto unknown genus within this supra-generic grouping.\nL52: On the basis of both phylogenetic and phenotypic evidence, it is proposed that the unknown bacterium from blood be classified in a new genus, Fastidiosipila gen. nov., as Fastidiosipila sanguinis sp. nov. The type strain of Fastidiosipila sanguinis is CCUG 47711 T (=CIP 108292 T).\nL53:   * Published Online: 01/03/2005\nL54: \nL55: SGM\nL56: \nL57: 【30†Image: Loading】\nL58: \nL59: Article metrics loading...\nL60: \nL61: /content/journal/ijsem/10.1099/ijs.0.63327-0\nL62: \nL63: 2005-03-01\nL64: \nL65: 2026-11-08\nL66: ## Metrics\nL67: \nL68: 【30†Image: Loading full text...】\nL69: \nL70: Full text loading...\nL71: \nL72: /deliver/fulltext/ijsem/55/2/ijs550853.html?itemId=/content/journal/ijsem/10.1099/ijs.0.63327-0&mimeType=html&fmt=ahah\nL73: \nL74: References\nL75: \nL76:   1. Cato E. P., George W. L., Finegold S. M. 1986; Genus Clostridium Prazmowski 1880, 23^{AL} . In Bergey's Manual of Systematic Bacteriology vol. 2 pp  1141–1200 Edited by Sneath P. H. A., Mair N. S., Sharp M. E., Holt J. G. Baltimore: Williams & Wilkins;\nL77: 【31†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL78:   2. Collins M. D., Lawson P. A., Willems A., Cordoba J. J., Fernandez-Garayzabal J., Garcia P., Cai J., Hippe H., Farrow J. E. A. 1994; The phylogeny of the genus Clostridium : proposal of five new genera and eleven new species combinations. Int J Syst Bacteriol 44:812–826 【32†[CrossRef]†dx.doi.org】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nClostridium scindens sp. nov., a Human Intestinal Bacterium with Desmolytic Activity on Corticoids | Microbiology Society (https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/content/journal/ijsem/10.1099/00207713-35-4-478)\nciteturn55view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/content/journal/ijsem/10.1099/00207713-35-4-478\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 267\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: 1887\nL3: \nL4: 【1†Skip to content】\nL5: \nL6: [Input]\nL7: \nL8: 【2†International Journal of Systematic and Evolutionary Microbiology】 【3†Volume 35, Issue 4】\nL9: # Clostridium scindens sp. nov., a Human Intestinal Bacterium with Desmolytic Activity on Corticoids 【4†Image: Free】\nL10: \nL11:   * 【5†G. N. MORRIS】^{1}, 【6†J. WINTER】^{1}, 【7†E. P. CATO】^{2}, 【8†A. E. RITCHIE】^{3} and 【9†V. D. BOKKENHEUSER】^{1,*}\nL12:   * 【10†View Affiliations Hide Affiliations】\nL13: ^{1} 【11†Department of Pathology, St. Luke’s Hospital Center, New York, New York 10025】 ^{2} 【12†Department of Anaerobic Microbiology, Virginia Polytechnic Institute and State University, Blacksburg, Virginia 24061】 ^{3} 【13†The National Animal Disease Center, Ames, Iowa 50010】\nL14: \nL15: *Corresponding author.\nL16: \nL17:   * Published: 01 October 1985\nL18:   * 【10†info Info】\nL19:     * 【14†Article information】\nL20:     * 【15†Full-Text】\nL21:     * 【16†Figures and Tables】\nL22:     * 【17†References (25)】\nL23:     * 【18†Cited By (56)】\nL24:     * 【19†Supplementary Material】\nL25:     * 【20†Metrics】\nL26: \nL27:   * 【10†build Tools】\nL28: ### Tools\nL29: \nL30:     * 【10†Add to my favourites】\nL31: \nL32:     * 【10†Create publication alert】\nL33: \nL34: 【10†Create citation alert】\nL35: \nL36: 【10†Create correction alert】\nL37: \nL38:     * 【10†Export citation】\nL39:       * 【21†BibT_{E}X】\nL40:       * 【22†Endnote】\nL41:       * 【21†Zotero】\nL42:       * 【23†Medlars】\nL43:       * 【24†RefWorks】\nL44:       * 【25†Mendeley†www.mendeley.com】\nL45:     * 【10†Recommend to library】\nL46:     *     * [Input]\nL47: ## Abstract\nL48: Clostridium scindens sp. nov., an obligate anaerobe with desmolytic activity, was isolated from human fecal flora. The desmolase, not associated previously with any specific intestinal microorganism, cleaves the carbon-carbon bond of 17-hydroxylated corticoids at C17-C20, thereby converting them to androstans (C19 steroids). In primary cultures on sheep blood agar plates, C. scindens forms minute, nonhemolytic colonies. The gram-positive rods (0.5 to 0.7 by 1 to 2.5 μm) are slightly curved.\nL49: The rare oval terminal spores (0.8 to 2.0 μm in diameter) are extremely difficult to demonstrate in Gram-stained smears. More than 40% of the cells are fimbriated. Neither a capsule nor flagella are present. d-Fructose, d-glucose, lactose, d-mannose, d-ribose, and d-xylose are fermented. The major fermentation products are acetic acid, ethanol, and hydrogen. The type strain is ATCC 35704 (Bokkenheuser strain 19).\nL50:   * Published Online: 01/10/1985\nL51: \nL52: Copyright © 1985 International Union of Microbiological Societies\nL53: \nL54: 【26†Image: Loading】\nL55: \nL56: Article metrics loading...\nL57: \nL58: /content/journal/ijsem/10.1099/00207713-35-4-478\nL59: \nL60: 1985-10-01\nL61: \nL62: 2026-11-01\nL63: ## Metrics\nL64: \nL65: 【26†Image: Loading full text...】\nL66: \nL67: Full text loading...\nL68: \nL69: /deliver/fulltext/ijsem/35/4/ijs-35-4-478.html?itemId=/content/journal/ijsem/10.1099/00207713-35-4-478&mimeType=html&fmt=ahah\nL70: \nL71: References\nL72: \nL73:   1. Bokkenheuser V. D., Morris G. N., Ritchie A. E., Holdeman L. V., Winter J. 1984; Biosynthesis of androgen from cortisol by a Clostridium species recovered from human fecal flora. J. Infect. Dis. 149:489–494\nL74: 【27†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL75:   2. Bokkenheuser V. D., Winter J., Cohen B. I., O’Rourke S., Mosbach E. H. 1983; Inactivation of contraceptive steroid hormones by human intestinal clostridia. J. Clin. Microbiol. 18:500–504\nL76: \nL77: 【28†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL78:   3. Bokkenheuser V. D., Winter J., Dehazya P., DeLeon O., Kelly W. G. 1976; Formation and metabolism of tetrahydro-deoxycorticosterone by human fecal flora. J. Steroid Biochem. 7:837–843\nL79: 【29†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL80:   4. Bokkenheuser V. D., Winter J., Dehazya P., Kelly W. G. 1977; Isolation and characterization of human fecal bacteria capable of 21-dehydroxylating corticoids. Appl. Environ. Microbiol. 34:571–575\nL81: \nL82: 【30†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL83:   5. Cerone-McLernon A. M., Winter J., Mosbach E. H., Bokkenheuser V. D. 1981; Side-chain cleavage of cortisol by fecal flora. Biochim. Biophys. Acta 666:341–347\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTreponema pectinovorum sp. nov. Isolated from Humans with Periodontitis | Microbiology Society (https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/content/journal/ijsem/10.1099/00207713-33-4-852)\nciteturn55view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/content/journal/ijsem/10.1099/00207713-33-4-852\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 230\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: 1887\nL3: \nL4: 【1†Skip to content】\nL5: \nL6: [Input]\nL7: \nL8: 【2†International Journal of Systematic and Evolutionary Microbiology】 【3†Volume 33, Issue 4】\nL9: # Treponema pectinovorum sp. nov. Isolated from Humans with Periodontitis 【4†Image: Free】\nL10: \nL11:   * 【5†R. M. SMIBERT】^{1} and 【6†J. A. BURMEISTER】^{2}\nL12:   * 【7†View Affiliations Hide Affiliations】\nL13: \nL14: ^{1} 【8†Department of Anaerobic Microbiology, Virginia Polytechnic Institute and State University, Blacksburg, Virginia 24061】 ^{2} 【9†Department of Peridontology, College of Dentistry, Virginia Commonwealth University, Richmond, Virginia 23298】\nL15: \nL16:   * Published: 01 October 1983\nL17:   * 【7†info Info】\nL18:     * 【10†Article information】\nL19:     * 【11†Full-Text】\nL20:     * 【12†Figures and Tables】\nL21:     * 【13†References (10)】\nL22:     * 【14†Cited By (72)】\nL23:     * 【15†Supplementary Material】\nL24:     * 【16†Metrics】\nL25: \nL26:   * 【7†build Tools】\nL27: ### Tools\nL28: \nL29:     * 【7†Add to my favourites】\nL30: \nL31:     * 【7†Create publication alert】\nL32: \nL33: 【7†Create citation alert】\nL34: \nL35: 【7†Create correction alert】\nL36: \nL37:     * 【7†Export citation】\nL38:       * 【17†BibT_{E}X】\nL39:       * 【18†Endnote】\nL40:       * 【17†Zotero】\nL41:       * 【19†Medlars】\nL42:       * 【20†RefWorks】\nL43:       * 【21†Mendeley†www.mendeley.com】\nL44:     * 【7†Recommend to library】\nL45:     *     * [Input]\nL46: ## Abstract\nL47: A new species of Treponema, Treponema pectinovorum, which was isolated from supragingival and subgingival samples from patients with periodontitis, is described. This new anaerobic species requires rumen fluid or short-chain volatile fatty acids and a fermentable energy source. Pectin, polygalacturonic acid, glucuronic acid, and galacturonic acid are the only substrates fermented. The main end products of fermentation are acetic and formic acids; traces of pyruvic and lactic acids occasionally are found.\nL48: The type strain of T. pectinovorum is strain VPI D-36DR-2 (= ATCC 33768). The guanine-plus-cytosine content of the deoxyribonucleic acid of the type strain is 39 mol%.\nL49:   * Published Online: 01/10/1983\nL50: \nL51: Copyright © 1983 International Union of Microbiological Societies\nL52: \nL53: 【22†Image: Loading】\nL54: \nL55: Article metrics loading...\nL56: \nL57: /content/journal/ijsem/10.1099/00207713-33-4-852\nL58: \nL59: 1983-10-01\nL60: \nL61: 2026-10-28\nL62: ## Metrics\nL63: \nL64: 【22†Image: Loading full text...】\nL65: \nL66: Full text loading...\nL67: \nL68: /deliver/fulltext/ijsem/33/4/ijs-33-4-852.html?itemId=/content/journal/ijsem/10.1099/00207713-33-4-852&mimeType=html&fmt=ahah\nL69: \nL70: References\nL71: \nL72:   1. Cwyk W. M., Canale-Parola E. 1979; Treponema succinifaciens sp. nov.: an ancrobic spirochete from the swine intestine. Arch. Microbiol 122:231–239\nL73: 【23†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL74:   2